 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Front Hemisphere Constraint:** The method is only applicable if all samples lie strictly in the front hemisphere of the average camera frame [S2].
-*   **Perspective Distortions:** Large deviations from the average camera frame lead to high non-linear perspective distortions, which may negatively impact the learning process [S2].
-*   **Sample Count Threshold:** In ablation studies, NDC sampling only surpasses uniform sampling in quality at 128 samples [S2]. It performs worse than all other local sampling approaches until $N=32$ [S2].
+*   **Average Camera Frame:** The method is only applicable if all samples lie strictly in the front hemisphere of the average camera frame. Large deviations from this frame lead to high non-linear perspective distortions, which can negatively impact the learning process [S2].
+*   **Ray Direction Norm:** In NDC space, the ray direction norm may vary. If Euclidean distance accuracy is required, distances must be scaled by the ray direction norm [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Bounding Box:** If the bounding box is not provided, the method does not filter out-of-bounds points, which may lead to sampling empty space or invalid regions [S3, unofficial].
