+## Subtopics
+
+### NDC Ray Sampling & Distance Scaling
+A variant that computes inter-sample distances and scales them by the ray direction norm to approximate Euclidean distance in NDC space [S4, unofficial]. It uses parameters `ndc_near_plane` (default 1.0), `simulate_euclid_sample` (bool), and `simulate_euclid_depth` (float) to control depth simulation [S4, unofficial]. This variant is chosen when accurate depth-based weighting is needed despite using NDC sampling [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \times N_{samples})$ per batch, as it performs a fixed number of linear interpolations [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \times N_{samples})$ to store the sampled points and bounding box masks [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** In ablation studies on diverse scenes (Bulldozer, Forest, Classroom, San Miguel), NDC sampling only manages to surpass uniform sampling at 128 samples [S2]. It performs worse than all other local sampling approaches until $N=32$ [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Combines uniform and logarithmic sampling approaches from the perspective of network input [S2]. Linear in NDC space [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Limited to scenes where rays can be transformed to an average frame with all samples in the front hemisphere [S2]. Sensitive to large deviations from the average camera frame [S2].
-*   **When to Use:** When using a fixed number of samples per ray and the scene geometry allows for a stable average camera frame transformation [S2].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When rays extend into the back hemisphere of the average camera frame or when high accuracy is required with low sample counts (<128) [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \times N\_samples)$, where $N$ is the number of rays and $N\_samples$ is the number of samples per ray. The computation is simple linear interpolation and is highly parallelizable on GPUs [S1, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \times N\_samples \times 3)$ for storing the sampled points [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** NDC sampling only surpasses uniform sampling in quality at 128 samples in ablation studies [S2]. It is generally less efficient than stratified methods for low sample counts [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Consistent sample distribution regardless of scene scale; simple implementation; compatible with positional encoding that expects NDC inputs [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires transformation to an average camera frame; sensitive to large deviations from the average frame; less effective than logarithmic or stratified sampling for foreground-focused scenes with low sample counts [S2].
+*   **When to Use:** When using positional encodings designed for NDC space; when scene scale varies significantly; when a fixed number of samples is required regardless of depth [S2].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When high accuracy at low sample counts is needed; when the scene cannot be easily transformed to an average camera frame; when depth-aware sampling (e.g., logarithmic) is preferred [S2].
 
 ## Background
-Uniform NDC Point Sampling was introduced in the context of NeRF (Neural Radiance Fields) [S2]. It is based on the NDC sampling strategy proposed in the original NeRF paper [MST*20] [S2]. The method transforms rays into an average camera frame and uniformly samples within the projected normalized device coordinates (NDC), directly feeding NDC samples into positional encoding [S2]. The detailed derivations for the transformation are provided in the appendix of the original NeRF paper [S2].
+Uniform NDC Point Sampling was introduced in the context of NeRF (Neural Radiance Fields) to address the issue of positional encoding sensitivity to scene scale and camera pose [S2]. The method transforms rays into an average camera frame and samples uniformly in NDC space, allowing the network to learn consistent features regardless of the original camera configuration [S2]. It was proposed as an alternative to logarithmic sampling, offering a linear sampling curve in NDC space that corresponds to a $1/x$ disparity distribution in original space [S2]. The approach is detailed in the original NeRF paper and subsequent works like DONeRF [S2].
 
